17.a5 Nf4 18.Na4 Bd7 19.Ndc3
{ Critical moment: Black to move. This earlier DCC example is not a unique-best proof. The 2026-09-29 saved snapshot tied Bxa4 and Rad8 at −0.10 (Black POV); ten-ply trajectory heuristics then preferred Bxa4, but only 3 of 47 legal moves had root scores. A later, separate deep review favored Nxe2+/Rad8 over Bxa4; Stockfish d36 slightly preferred Nxe2+. This position was pre-revealed in the review, so it is not a blind discovery. Keep it as a dated DCC-method example, not evidence that DCC+ beat Kasparov. }
19... Bxa4 20.Rxa4
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD proposed 20...Reb8 before seeing evaluations: the e-rook supports b7 while the a-rook stays on the a-file. Kasparov played 20...Rac8. Frozen R4 refreshed_02 (learn=0) scores Reb8 0 cp for Black; baseline also showed 0. The same R4 answer does not directly score Rac8, so it is not a fresh CDB head-to-head. A separate Stockfish d34 run scores Reb8 0.00 vs Rac8 −0.42 (Black POV). This supports the idea in that search. 22...Rb8 is a different position and a different choice. }
20... Rac8 21.Kh1 c4 22.Rb4
{ Critical moment: Black to move. Before evaluation, BD listed Qd8, Re7, Qe7 and Rb8, then chose Rb8; Kasparov played Nxe2. Separate Stockfish d32 (Black POV): Rc7 0.00, Nd3 −0.09, Re7 −0.12, Qd8 −0.57, Qe7 −1.26, Nxe2 −1.35, Rb8 −2.37. Some BD candidates outrank the game move, but his final Rb8 does not. R4's published same-root CDB sample directly scores Rc7 at +1 cp only; do not merge it with this older SF comparison. }
22... Nxe2 23.Qxe2 Qe7 24.Rb6 Nd3 25.Qd2 f5 26.Bg1
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD considered f4 and fxe4; Kasparov played f4. Separate Stockfish d33 (Black POV) instead favors the quiet rook lift: Rf8 0.00, f4 −0.90, fxe4 −1.19. The R4 report does not publish a comparable root sample for this choice. The lesson is candidate generation: when two pawn moves look natural, also search for a different kind of improving move. }
26... f4 27.b3 Ne5 28.Rb4 cxb3 29.Rxb3
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD saw Nc4 and Qc7, then chose Qc7; Kasparov played Nc4. Separate Stockfish d28 (Black POV) scores Nc4 +1.00 vs Qc7 −0.65; the CDB review also favored Nc4. The knight attacks Qd2 with tempo. Playing Qc7 first lets White move the queen and gives up that tempo. Kasparov later played ...Qc7 after 30.Qa2, but that is a new position; it does not make the earlier move order equivalent. }
29... Nc4 30.Qa2 Qc7 31.e5
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD had Rxe5 in his candidate list before evaluation, but chose Bxe5, as Kasparov did. Frozen R4 learn=0 reports Rxe5 +216 cp from Black's perspective (baseline +176); it does not publish Bxe5 in the same answer. Separate Stockfish d32 compares Rxe5 +1.98 with Bxe5 +1.40 (Black POV). The cross-source agreement supports the rook capture, but only the SF run is a direct two-move comparison. This was a ranking miss, not a missed candidate. }
31... Bxe5 32.Ne4
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD listed Nxa5 and Qxa5, then chose Nxa5 after GPT confirmed the candidate set; mark this as AI-influenced, not a clean blind choice. R4's frozen final answer scores Qxa5 +291 cp (Black POV), not a same-answer comparison with Nxa5. Separate Stockfish d31 gives Qxa5 +2.90 vs Nxa5 −0.07. After Nxa5, 33.Bb6 attacks Qc7 and the knight on a5 at once. Qxa5 removes the queen from c7 while Nc4 controls b6, so the same fork is unavailable. }
32... Nxa5 33.Rbb1 Nc4 34.Rfc1
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD's 34...Qa5 had already been revealed by GPT; do not label it a blind miss. In frozen R4, Qa5 moved from +412 cp in baseline to +383 cp in refreshed_02 (Black POV): distinct snapshots, not a causal trend. Separate Stockfish d36 directly compares Qa5 +4.18 with the played b5 +0.26. Qa5 attacks Qa2 with tempo while Nc4 controls b6; one saved line is 35.Qb3 Qxd5. These readings come from different searches and dates. }
34... b5 35.Qxa6 Qf7 36.Qxb5
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD found 36...Na3 in about 15 seconds before evaluation; Kasparov played it. The knight attacks Qb5 and Rb1 at once. Separate Stockfish d27 (Black POV) scores Na3 +2.98, compared with Ne3 0.00 and Nb2 −6.45. Treat the human discovery and engine ranking as separate evidence; neither is a game result or a claim of beating Kasparov. }
36... Na3 37.Qa6
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD mentioned Rxc1 and Nxb1, initially leaned toward Rxc1, but did not confirm a final choice; GPT then revealed Ra8. This is not a blind-find test. Separate Stockfish d33 (Black POV): Ra8 +3.27, Nxb1 −4.59, Rxc1 −7.00. Ra8 moves the c8-rook out of the exchange tactic and attacks Qa6 with tempo. In the game, 37...Ra8 38.Qd3 Nxb1 39.Rxb1 wins the exchange. Immediate capture misses the queen-tempo that makes the knight capture safe. }
37... Ra8 38.Qd3 Nxb1 39.Rxb1 Re7 40.h3 Qg6 41.Kh2
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD wanted h5 one move earlier than Kasparov. At this exact position after 41.Kh2, separate Stockfish d36 (Black POV) scores h5 +5.36 vs the played Rf8 +4.06. The R4 report does not publish a same-root score for this moment, so this is SF-only support. Kasparov plays ...h5 after 42.Rb6; that later position is not a direct comparison with the earlier h5 idea. }
41... Rf8 42.Rb6 h5 43.Qa6 g4 44.Nxd6 gxf3 45.Qf1
{ Critical moment: Black to move. BD chose fxg2 before evaluation; Kasparov played Bxd6. In frozen R4, fxg2 changed from +1865 cp in baseline to raw 29973 in refreshed_02. This is a CDB encoded mate score, not 29,973 cp: 30000 − 29973 = 27 plies, about M14 with Black to move. It is the provider's forced-mate result, not a formal proof of every defense. A separate older Stockfish d34 search also reports M14 and stores this legal line: 45...fxg2 46.Qf3 Qd3 47.Nf5 Qxf3 48.Rg6+ Kf7 49.Rg5 Qxd5 50.h4 f3+ 51.Ng3 Ke8 52.Bf2 Bxg3+ 53.Bxg3 g1=Q+ 54.Kxg1 Qd1+ 55.Kh2 Re2+ 56.Bf2 Rxf2+ 57.Kg3 Qg1+ 58.Kh3 Qh2#. R4 itself ran no local Stockfish jobs. }
